== DC Circuits

#entry($V = I R quad P = I V = I^2 R = frac(V^2, R)$, note: [
    Series: $R_"eq" = sum R_i$. Parallel: $1\/R_"eq" = sum 1\/R_i$.
])

== Faraday's Law & Induction

#entry($cal(E) = -frac(d Phi_B, d t) = -integral.cont bold(E) dot d bold(s) quad cal(E)_L = -L frac(d I, d t)$, note: [
    Lenz: induced current opposes flux change. Motional EMF: $cal(E) = B L v$. Inductor opposes $Delta I$.
])

== RL & RC Circuits

#aheader[RL — charging][RC — charging]

#dual(
    $I(t) = frac(cal(E), R)(1 - e^(-t\/tau_L))$,
    $q(t) = C cal(E)(1 - e^(-t\/tau_C))$,
    note-left: [
        $tau_L = L\/R$. At $tau_L$: $I approx 63%$ of $cal(E)\/R$. \
        $U_L = frac(1,2)L I^2$. Mag. density: $u_B = B^2\/(2mu_0)$.
    ],
    note-right: [
        $tau_C = R C$. Discharge: $q = Q_0 e^(-t\/tau_C)$. \
        Loop: $cal(E) = I R + q\/C$. Elec. density: $u_E = frac(1,2)epsilon_0 E^2$.
    ],
)

== LC & RLC Oscillations

#entry($frac(d^2 q, d t^2) + frac(q, L C) = 0 quad => quad omega_0 = frac(1, sqrt(L C))$, note: [
    Ideal LC: energy oscillates between $frac(1,2)L I^2$ and $Q^2\/(2C)$ — analogous to mass-spring. \
    RLC (damped): $frac(d^2 q, d t^2) + frac(R, L) frac(d q, d t) + frac(q, L C) = 0$.
])


